is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the RIP routing configuration on router6 (FRR ripd). Router6 is the edge router for Location C, with LANs 172.18.50.0/24 (eth0) and 172.18.51.0/24 (eth1).

Mechanism: Router6 is not advertising its local LAN prefixes into RIP. Its FRR config only enables RIP on 10.0.0.0/8, which covers the WAN link 10.50.1.0/24 but not the 172.18.50/24 and 172.18.51/24 LANs. As a result, upstream routers never learn those LAN routes via RIP; instead they hold miscellaneous kernel/static routes that don’t refresh with RIP, so paths to Location C can appear and disappear as those non-RIP entries age out and reappear, matching the “routes aging out and being relearned” observation.

Localization: On router6 (ripd), interfaces eth0 (172.18.50.254/24) and eth1 (172.18.51.254/24) are not under RIP. Evidence: router6 FRR config shows “router rip” with only “network 10.0.0.0/8”; yet router6 has connected 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24. Other routers’ RIP tables lack R routes for 172.18.5x; pings to 172.18.51.15 (pc15) fail from router0/router3, but succeed from router6, confirming reachability only locally.

Root cause: Misconfiguration—router6 is not advertising its 172.18.50/24 and 172.18.51/24 LANs into RIP.

Corrective fix: On router6, add those LANs to RIP (e.g., in vtysh: router rip; network 172.18.50.0/24; network 172.18.51.0/24; version 2) or use “network 172.18.0.0/16” and write memory. Remove any stray static/kernel routes elsewhere to avoid confusion. This will provide stable, refreshed routes to Location C across the WAN.
=== END ===